From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] media: i2c: add a driver for the Sony IMX563

The Sony IMX563 is the rear wide camera sensor of the Samsung Galaxy Z
Flip3 (SM-F711N). It sends 10-bit Bayer data over a MIPI CSI-2 C-PHY
link of three trios and is controlled over I2C at 0x10, with the chip
ID 0x0563 at 0x0016.

No datasheet was available. The init list and the mode lists are copied
from the sensor module that the phone's stock camera stack loads
(com.samsung.sensormodule.0_sony_imx563_DV0_DV1; the DV2 module has the
same sensor data), in its order and with its values; only comments were
added. The modes are the stock ones at 1896 Msps per trio:

- 4000x3000, 30 fps (stock mode 0)
- 4000x2252, 30 fps (stock mode 1) or 60 fps (stock mode 2)

Stock's modes 1 and 2 have the same size and the same list except for
the frame length (8002 or 4000 lines) and the initial exposure, so they
are one mode here: the vertical blanking control sets the frame length,
from stock's 60 fps value up. The default is stock's 30 fps.

Both modes read out the full 4032 pixel width and crop it to 4000
columns digitally, starting at column 16. 4000x3000 reads rows 12 to
3011; 4000x2252 reads rows 384 to 2639 and crops from row 386. The
crop selection reports these windows. The stock modes at 2297.6 and
2944 Msps are left out, and so is stock's mode 8, whose frame length
does not match its frame rate.

The OP PLL in every list gives 19.2 MHz / 2 * 395 / 2 = 1896 Msps per
trio, the base entry of the stock kernel's adaptive MIPI table for this
sensor. The link frequency is half the symbol rate, 948 MHz, and the
endpoint must list it.

The pixel rate is 4121.6 Mpixel/s: the VT PLL is 19.2 MHz / 3 * 322 =
2060.8 MHz in every list, and line length times frame length times
frame rate gives twice that in every stock mode. That is the rate that
makes line length / pixel rate the line time. The CSI-2 output rate is
lower, 1296.864 Mpixel/s by stock's figure.

The controls are exposure (10 lines up to frame length - 96), analogue
gain (codes 0 to 896, gain = 1024 / (1024 - code), up to 8x, as stock's
sensor library computes it), digital gain (Q8, 0x100 to 0xfd7, stock's
15.84x cap), vblank, read-only hblank, pixel rate and link frequency,
and the SMIA/CCS test pattern at 0x0600 with its four colour registers.
Stock writes 0xb000 = 0 with every pattern change, and so does the
driver. There are no flip controls: stock never writes the orientation
register.

The lists leave the HDR mode (0x0220) off, so the short exposure and
gain registers they seed are not driven. Stock describes a second
stream on virtual channel 1 for these modes, which looks like PDAF data;
only the image on virtual channel 0 is described.

The media bus code is SRGGB10. The stock module gives CFA code 3 for
every mode, as the IMX258 module does, and mainline's imx258 driver
reports the unflipped IMX258 as RGGB. Code 2 in the IMX374 module was
confirmed as GRBG on the phone. All crop offsets are even, so the order
is the same in both modes.

Power-up follows stock: an extra 1.8 V rail (stock's cam_v_custom1),
VANA, VDIG and VIF 1 ms apart, XCLR released, MCLK 2 ms later, then
18 ms before the first access. The binding calls the extra rail
vcustom. The init list programs EXCK for 19.2 MHz, so probe rejects any
other clock rate. The endpoint is parsed as C-PHY, so one that says
D-PHY is refused.

